      {
        "ContentType": "Description",
        "SortOrder": 0,
        "Content": "\nAn exceptional mountain estate, elevated above Dublin City overlooking Bohernabreena Reservoir and set within more than 220 acres of dramatic landscape.\n\nApproached by a long, winding gravel avenue and framed by mature Scots pines, beech, mountain ash and colourful rhododendron, the house enjoys complete privacy and uninterrupted views across the Glenasmole Valley down to Bohernabreena Reservoir. \n\nDespite its remarkable seclusion, the property lies about 17 kilometres from Dublin City Centre, with Dublin Airport typically accessible in within an hour's drive. It is a rare combination of scale, tranquillity and accessibility.\n\nA house of long established character, Glenasmole Lodge originated as a Georgian sporting lodge in the late eighteenth century, chosen for its commanding position at the head of the valley. Over the centuries it has been sensitively adapted and enhanced, resulting in a home that blends architectural charm with a warm, welcoming atmosphere. \n\nThe accommodation is elegant, light filled and arranged in a natural flow that suits both family living and entertaining. Many rooms enjoy dual or triple aspects, with long views over the gardens, valley and surrounding mountains. A Gothic panelled door opens into an entrance porch with ornate cornicing and parquet flooring, leading to a generous staircase hall with a carved pine staircase rising to the first floor.\n\nThe principal reception rooms are beautifully proportioned and rich in period detail. The drawing room is a superb dual aspect space with matching bow windows and a fine marble fireplace, ideal for formal entertaining. The library features part panelled walls, bookcases with carved columns, a large marble fireplace and an ornate Venetian window, with a door opening directly to the long veranda. The study, with its bow window and door to a garden terrace, offers a private workspace. The dining room is distinguished by a carved pine fireplace and an ornate arch with carved pediment, leading into the sunroom, where Gothic style windows and double French doors open to the gardens and create a bright, year round living space.\n\nAt the heart of the house lies the country kitchen, a generous and welcoming room with painted timber units, an Aga cooker and ample space for a large dining table. Its direct access to the garden makes it ideal for informal family living, outdoor dining and entertaining. A long ornamental veranda supported by antique cast iron columns spans the front elevation and provides a magnificent outdoor entertaining area overlooking the valley.\n\nThe first floor is arranged around a central vestibule and offers five bedrooms in total, four of which are ensuite. The master suite enjoys exceptional views across the valley, generous wardrobe space and a well appointed bathroom, while the principal guest suite mirrors its quality and comfort. Two further bedrooms have their own ensuite bathrooms. A secondary staircase links the bedroom floor to the library, providing an alternative circulation route typical of large period houses.\n\nThe pleasure grounds extend to approximately 34.3 acres and are designed in the Robinsonian style, where exotic trees and shrubs are planted to appear naturally rooted in the landscape. Seasonal interest is exceptional, with colour and structure throughout the year. Formal elements include a large south facing terrace, a kidney shaped pond, and a tennis court. Beyond the pleasure grounds, the estate rises into more than 220 acres of mountain land, linking directly to commonage with extensive walking and bridle routes.\n\nA yard east of the house includes stabling, an old coach house and a former staff cottage with belfry, now used for storage or garaging. The Bell House offers excellent potential as additional accommodation or a home office, subject to planning. A sand arena enjoys one of the most dramatic equestrian settings in the region, with useful grazing adjoining the house and connecting to the wider lands."
